---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { loadFeed } from '../lib/feed.ts';
import { computedGaps } from '../lib/derive.ts';

const feed = await loadFeed();
const { skills, decisions, manifest, findings } = feed;
const computed = computedGaps(feed).length;
---

<Layout
  title="Methods"
  section="methods"
  description="The calculators behind every figure on this site, and the decisions that shape the corpus."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Methods</p>
  <h1>How a figure gets onto this site</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Nothing here is computed in the browser, and nothing is computed by this site's own code.
    Every number on every page is emitted by a calculator in <code>crates/</code>, exported
    as JSON, and rendered. The site formats figures; it does not produce them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the arithmetic lives on the other side</h2>

  <p>
    Differencing two numbers is easy. Deciding <em>whether</em> to difference them is the
    part that carries the domain, and it is written down in the skills below: that an
    <span class="claim claim--open">[open]</span> amount is absence rather than zero, that a
    recipient's share may not be measured against a whole line item's authority, that
    expenditures reported on different bases may not be summed, that no comparison spanning
    fiscal periods is valid without a deflator.
  </p>

  <p>
    Reimplementing those rules in TypeScript would create a second set of them. The two would
    drift, silently, in the direction of whichever was easier to write — and the web copy is
    always the easier one, because it is the one under pressure to render something. So this
    site consumes verdicts, including the refusals. Where a page says no figure was drawn,
    that is a calculator's judgment reaching the reader intact.
  </p>

  <dl class="figures">
    <div>
      <dt>Feed contract</dt>
      <dd>v{manifest.contract_version}<small>commit {manifest.commit}</small></dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Gap pairs</dt>
      <dd>{computed}/{findings.gap.length}<small>computable</small></dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Stage series</dt>
      <dd>{findings.stage_delta.length}<small>decomposable</small></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <div class="refusal">
    <div class="refusal__label">Constant dollars</div>
    <p style="margin:0">{manifest.real_dollars.reason}</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Calculators</h2>
  <p>
    Each is a procedure that emerged from inquiry rather than one written in advance. The
    rules a calculator refuses under are as much a part of it as the arithmetic.
  </p>

  {
    skills.map((s) => (
      <div class="card" id={`skill-${s.slug}`} style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
        <div class="card__path">{s.path}</div>
        <div class="card__title" style="font-size:var(--text-lg)">{s.name}</div>
        <p style="margin:0;font-size:var(--text-sm);color:var(--text-secondary)">
          {s.description}
        </p>
      </div>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Decisions</h2>
  <p>
    Why the corpus is shaped the way it is. A decision record exists so that a choice made
    once is not silently remade later by someone who did not know it had been settled.
  </p>

  {
    decisions.map((d) => (
      <div class="card" id={`decision-${d.id}`} style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">
        <div class="card__path">.yidam/decisions/{d.id}.yml</div>
        <div class="card__title" style="font-size:var(--text-lg)">{d.summary}</div>
        <details>
          <summary
            style="cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:var(--text-sm);color:var(--text-secondary)"
          >
            Context, decision, and rationale
          </summary>
          <div style="font-size:var(--text-sm);margin-top:var(--space-4)">
            <h4>Context</h4>
            <Prose text={d.context} from={`.yidam/decisions/${d.id}.yml`} />
            <h4>Decision</h4>
            <Prose text={d.decision} from={`.yidam/decisions/${d.id}.yml`} />
            <h4>Rationale</h4>
            <Prose text={d.rationale} from={`.yidam/decisions/${d.id}.yml`} />
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Rebuilding this site</h2>
  <p>
    The JSON feed is generated, not committed — a second copy of the corpus that could
    disagree with it is exactly the failure the repository's gate exists to catch.
  </p>
  <pre style="background:var(--surface-overlay);padding:var(--space-4);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);overflow-x:auto"><code>mise run export   # corpus + calculators → web/src/data/*.json
mise run web-build</code></pre>
</Layout>
